The zlib sources in "zlib-1.2.13/" are the upstream release, plus one addition:
an x86_64 PCLMULQDQ / VPCLMULQDQ implementation of "crc32_z()", which takes
over from zlib's own table driven code at run time when the CPU supports it.

"zlib-1.2.13/README" is the upstream zlib README and has nothing to do with
this file.

Scope of the modification
-------------------------

New files, added by this fork (see the Origin section for authorship). None of
them shadows or rewrites an upstream zlib file, and together the five are 817
lines:

    zlib-1.2.13/arch_x86.c                CPU feature detection (CPUID/XGETBV)
    zlib-1.2.13/arch_x86.h
    zlib-1.2.13/crc32_x86.h               PCLMULQDQ/VPCLMULQDQ folding kernels
    zlib-1.2.13/crc32_x86_pclmul.c        AVX (128 bit) translation unit
    zlib-1.2.13/crc32_x86_vpclmulqdq.c    AVX-512/VPCLMULQDQ translation unit

Modified upstream files, 48 added lines in "crc32.c" and 15 in "CMakeLists.txt".
The delta is small enough to list in full. It is guarded by one of two
conditions: the architecture macros "__x86_64__" and "__amd64__", which decide
whether the addition exists at all, and "ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL", which
"arch_x86.h" defines and which guards the code that actually uses the kernels.

    "zlib-1.2.13/crc32.c", five hunks

      - after the "#include \"zutil.h\"" line, the header of the addition,
        guarded by "__x86_64__" / "__amd64__" so that
        "ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL" stays undefined on every other target:

            #if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__amd64__)
            #include "arch_x86.h"
            #endif

      - the "DYNAMIC_CRC_TABLE" declaration block, moved from just after
        "#define POLY 0xedb88320" to just after the "#endif" that closes
        "once()". A pure relocation, not one line changed, so it carries no
        guard. The Re-applying section below says why the move is needed.

      - after the closing brace of "get_crc_table()", guarded by
        "ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL", the dispatch state:

            #ifdef ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL
            static unsigned long (*crc32_x86_fp)(unsigned long crc, const unsigned char FAR* buf,
                                                 z_size_t len) = NULL;
            local once_t crc32_x86_once = ONCE_INIT;

            static void crc32_x86_init(void) {
                crc32_x86_fp = NULL;
            #ifdef HAVE_VPCLMULQDQ_ATTRIBUTE
                if (has_crc32_x86_avx512())
                    crc32_x86_fp = crc32_z_impl_x86_avx512;
                else
            #endif
                if (has_crc32_x86_avx())
                    crc32_x86_fp = crc32_z_impl_x86_avx;
            }

            static void crc32_x86_setup_native(void) {
                once(&crc32_x86_once, crc32_x86_init);
            }
            #endif /* ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL */

      - in "crc32_z()", after the "if (buf == Z_NULL) return 0;" early return:

            #ifdef ZLIB_X86_64_CRC32_PCLMUL
                crc32_x86_setup_native();
                if (crc32_x86_fp != NULL)
                    return crc32_x86_fp(crc, buf, len);
            #endif

        It is the only call site, and when "crc32_x86_fp" is still NULL the
        function falls through to the table driven code below it.

    "zlib-1.2.13/CMakeLists.txt", one hunk, 15 lines

      - after the "set(ZLIB_SRCS ...)" list and before "if(NOT MINGW)":

            check_c_source_compiles("
                #if !defined(__x86_64__) && !defined(__amd64__)
                #  error not an x86_64 target
                #endif
                int main(void) { return 0; }" ZLIB_TARGET_X86_64)

            if(ZLIB_TARGET_X86_64)
                LIST(APPEND ZLIB_PRIVATE_HDRS arch_x86.h
                                              crc32_x86.h)

                LIST(APPEND ZLIB_SRCS arch_x86.c
                                      crc32_x86_pclmul.c
                                      crc32_x86_vpclmulqdq.c)
            endif()

The same anchors appear in the Re-applying section below, with the number of
lines each hunk adds.

The addition is covered by "unittest/gunit/mysys_my_checksum-t.cc", the only
file outside "extra/zlib/" that names the added symbols. It declares
"has_crc32_x86_avx()", "has_crc32_x86_avx512()" and "crc32_z_impl_x86_avx",
declares "crc32_z_impl_x86_avx512" weak, and calls the two implementations
directly next to "crc32_z()", so it exercises the kernels and the dispatch
both. That file lies outside "extra/zlib/", so no grep over that directory
shows its delta, which is three architecture guards and three compiler guards:

    #if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__amd64__)
        the extern "C" block declaring those four symbols
        VerifyAgainstTableAndZlib()
        the six SIMD tests: Crc32ZMatchesAvxImplementationWhenSupported,
        Crc32ZDispatchesToAvxImplementationWhenSupported,
        ShortLengthWithNonZeroSeedMatchesReference,
        SixteenByteBoundaryWithVariousOffsets, and, under the compiler guard
        below, Crc32ZDispatchesToAvx512ImplementationWhenSupported and
        Crc32ZMatchesAvx512ImplementationWhenSupported

    #if defined(__clang__) || (defined(__GNUC__) && (__GNUC__ >= 8))
        the weak declaration of crc32_z_impl_x86_avx512
        the skip inside Crc32ZDispatchesToAvxImplementationWhenSupported, for
        the case where crc32_z already takes the AVX-512 path
        Crc32ZDispatchesToAvx512ImplementationWhenSupported
        Crc32ZMatchesAvx512ImplementationWhenSupported

Those two compiler guards name "crc32_z_impl_x86_avx512", which exists only
where "HAVE_VPCLMULQDQ_ATTRIBUTE" in "arch_x86.h" is set, so they carry the
same condition as that macro. Because the declaration is weak, a build that
leaves the AVX-512 kernel out still links: the symbol resolves to NULL, both
AVX-512 tests skip on it, and the AVX (128 bit) tests keep running against
"crc32_z_impl_x86_avx".

Re-applying after a zlib upgrade
--------------------------------

The five new files listed above are the ones to carry over; they do not depend
on the zlib version. They include only "zutil.h", the generated "crc32.h"
tables and standard or compiler intrinsic headers, so they can be copied
unchanged next to a newer zlib. The same addition has already been applied on
top of zlib 1.3.1, where none of the five needed a change for the version. The
two upstream files, "crc32.c" and "CMakeLists.txt", have to be patched again at
the anchors below; locate them by the upstream text they attach to, never by
line number.

    file            anchor                                             lines
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    crc32.c         after  #include "zutil.h" ... FAR definitions */      4
    crc32.c         move the DYNAMIC_CRC_TABLE declaration block that
                    follows  #define POLY 0xedb88320  down to just after
                    the #endif that closes the once() definition        17
    crc32.c         after the closing brace of get_crc_table()          21
    crc32.c         in crc32_z(), after  if (buf == Z_NULL) return 0;    6
    CMakeLists.txt  after the set(ZLIB_SRCS ...) list, before
                    if(NOT MINGW)                                      15

"lines" is the number of lines the hunk adds. The relocation counts twice, 17
lines removed where the block used to be and 17 added where it goes.

The relocation in "crc32.c" needs a note, since it is the only hunk that
touches code it does not add. In zlib 1.2.13 the "once()" helper is compiled
only when "DYNAMIC_CRC_TABLE" is defined, and the default bundled build does
not define it, because the CRC tables come from the checked in "crc32.h". The
dispatch state needs the same one time, thread safe initialisation, so instead
of adding a second once mechanism the declaration block was moved below
"once()", which makes the existing helper compile unconditionally. It is a pure
move: nothing inside the block changed, the "DYNAMIC_CRC_TABLE" build keeps the
same helper, the same declarations and the same state object, and only the
point at which that region opens has moved 17 lines down.

Platforms
---------

The SIMD code is used only when three things hold at once: the target is
x86_64, the compiler can build the code, and the CPU and the operating system
support the instructions. The first two are settled at build time, by whoever
can answer them, and the third at run time.

At build time, the target. "crc32.c" includes "arch_x86.h" when "__x86_64__" or
"__amd64__" is defined, and "zlib-1.2.13/CMakeLists.txt" asks the compiler the
same question through "check_c_source_compiles", caching the answer in
"ZLIB_TARGET_X86_64"; only when that is true are the three sources and the two
headers appended to "ZLIB_SRCS" and "ZLIB_PRIVATE_HDRS". Both tests read the
compiler's target rather than the machine CMake runs on, which is what makes a
cross build or an "-m32" build come out right, and it is also why the source
test and the CMake test cannot disagree with each other. On any other target
none of the added sources are compiled, and "crc32.c" is the upstream file, the
bundled build defines neither "DYNAMIC_CRC_TABLE" nor "ARMCRC32" and takes its
CRC from the checked in tables.

At build time, the compiler. gcc and clang both work: they define the macros
above, they accept the "__attribute__ ((target (...)))" that "crc32_x86.h" puts
on each kernel, and they accept the GNU style inline asm that "arch_x86.c" uses
for cpuid and xgetbv. clang defines "__x86_64__" for every x86-64 target, a
Windows one included, so the tests above admit it. cl.exe is not supported: it
defines "_M_X64" rather than "__x86_64__", so the tests above already leave it
out, and it provides neither of those extensions. Within gcc and clang, one
kernel asks for more than the other. The AVX (128 bit) translation unit needs
only the "avx,pclmul" target attribute, while "crc32_x86_vpclmulqdq.c" needs
"avx,pclmul,avx512f,vpclmulqdq", which "arch_x86.h" tests as
"HAVE_VPCLMULQDQ_ATTRIBUTE" (gcc 8 or later, or clang); that macro is the only
compiler version floor in the addition. Without it that file compiles to a
placeholder instead of a kernel, "crc32_z_impl_x86_avx512" does not exist, and
only the AVX path is registered.

At run time, the CPU and the operating system. The check runs once per process,
out of the first "crc32_z()" call. "has_crc32_x86_avx()" reads CPUID leaf 1 for
PCLMULQDQ, AVX and OSXSAVE, then XCR0 for the YMM state;
"has_crc32_x86_avx512()" reads CPUID leaf 1 for OSXSAVE, XCR0 for the YMM and
ZMM states, and CPUID leaf 7 for AVX512F, AVX512VL and VPCLMULQDQ. When neither
holds, "crc32_x86_fp" stays NULL and "crc32_z()" falls through to the code
below its dispatch, which is the upstream behaviour.

Origin
------

The SIMD kernels implement the folding CRC32 algorithm of the Intel
implementation, whose notices "crc32_x86.h" carries: copyright (C) 2013 Intel
Corporation, copyright (C) 2016 Marian Beermann (support for the initial
value), copyright Wangyang Guo (wangyang.guo@intel.com), plus "Modifications
Copyright (C) 2024-2025, Advanced Micro Devices" for the changes made here.
"crc32_x86_pclmul.c" and "crc32_x86_vpclmulqdq.c" carry copyright (C) 2022-2025,
Advanced Micro Devices, with the three clause BSD text. "arch_x86.c" and
"arch_x86.h" were written for this fork and carry copyright (c) 2026, Chengdu
Haiguang IC Design Co., Ltd.

Removing the modification
-------------------------

The addition is self contained and can be dropped at any time: revert the
commit that introduced it, which also removes the guards and the six tests it
adds to "unittest/gunit/mysys_my_checksum-t.cc". No file outside "extra/zlib/"
includes the added headers, and that unit test is the only file elsewhere that
names the added symbols.
